adjudication.rationale:
In `packages/app-store/googlecalendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`, the refreshed credential is computed as:

```ts
const key = parseRefreshTokenResponse(googleCredentials, googleCredentialSchema);
await prisma.credential.update({ data: { key } });
```

But `parseRefreshTokenResponse` (new file `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`) returns the *result object* of `zod.safeParse(...)` (a `SafeParseReturnType` with `{ success: true, data: ... }`), not the parsed `.data` itself:

```ts
const refreshTokenResponse = schema.safeParse(response);
...
return refreshTokenResponse;
```

So `key` will be stored as `{ success: true, data: {...parsed credentials...} }` rather than just the credential payload, corrupting the stored credential shape on refresh.

In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, if the parsed token data lacks `refresh_token`, it is forcibly set to the literal string `"refresh_token"`:
```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```
This becomes a real defect where callers merge the returned data into stored credentials. For example in `Office365CalendarService`, `tokenResponse.data` is spread into `o365AuthCredentials`:
```ts
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
```
If the provider’s refresh response omits `refresh_token` (common), this code will overwrite a previously valid stored refresh token with the placeholder string, breaking future refreshes.
